{
  "id": "galileo-sar-jamming",
  "title": "Galileo SAR link under jamming (CTMC)",
  "chapter": "Part 5 — Case study 2: Galileo SAR under jamming",
  "chapterOrder": 5,
  "formalism": "ctmc",
  "tool": "PRISM 4.x (or PRISM-games)",
  "slides": "Slides 39–42",
  "summary": "Four CTMC modules (Satellite, Signal, Attack, Defence) and one service formula. The jammer (SPARTA EX-0016) is countered by antenna nulling (CM0083) at rate λnull = α·λjam.",
  "description": [
    "Each module alternates between two states with exponential rates (slide 40): Satellite and Signal fail and recover; the Attack module switches between NO_ATTACK and JAMMED with λjam = 1/MTTA and μjam = 1/2; the Defence module activates nulling at λnull = α·λjam and stops at μnull = 1/2.",
    "The service is up when satellite and signal are healthy and either no attack is active, or nulling is active during the attack: formula system_up.",
    "α scales the defence rate relative to the attack rate (α = 1: same rate; α > 1: faster defence). REACTIVE = false keeps the four modules independent as on slide 40; REACTIVE = true lets nulling start only while the link is jammed.",
    "Nobody decides in this model (fixed attack and defence rates), which is why a CTMC is enough (slide 13).",
    "Rates span ten orders of magnitude (10⁻⁸ … 1): iterative steady-state solvers do not converge, so the properties use transient (U<=T, F<=T) and cumulative (C<=T) analysis."
  ],
  "objectives": [
    "Build a CTMC from rates (λ = 1/MTBF, μ = 1/MTTR) and a service formula.",
    "Write CSL properties: time-bounded until, steady state, cumulative rewards.",
    "Study the effect of α and of the attack frequency (MTTA) on resilience."
  ],
  "exercises": [
    "Run P=? [ \"up\" U<=T !\"up\" ] for α = 1, 2, 5, 10: are the curves parallel when α = 1?",
    "Increase MTTA (rarer attacks): how fast does the effect of jamming fade?",
    "Compare the expected defence reward with the 5.85 % defended/total signals of the GNU Radio simulation (slide 42).",
    "Set REACTIVE = true: what changes, and why?",
    "Check the long-run signal availability μsig / (λsig + μsig) with the slide-40 rates against the 99.22 % of slide 39. What do you conclude?"
  ],
